### JavaScript fetch example

```
fetch('https://example.com/notifications/123/dismiss', {
  method: 'DELETE',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': 'Bearer token123'
  }
})
.then(response => {
  if (response.status === 204) {
    console.log('Notification dismissed successfully');
    updateUI();
  } else if (response.ok) {
    return response.json();
  } else {
    throw new Error('Request failed');
  }
})
.catch(error => {
  console.error('Error:', error);
});
```
### Axios example

```
axios.delete('https://example.com/sessions/current')
  .then(response => {
    if (response.status === 204) {
      console.log('Logged out successfully');
      redirectToLogin();
    }
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error('Logout failed:', error);
  });
```
## FAQs

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| What does 204 mean? | Request succeeded with no content to return |
| When should I use 204? | For successful DELETE, PUT, or PATCH requests where no data needs to be returned |
| Can 204 have a response body? | No, it must be empty |
| Is 204 an error? | No, it’s a success code (2xx family) |
| Should I use 204 or 200? | Use 204 when there’s nothing to return, 200 when returning data adds value |

## 4. Handling HTTP 204 in a Client

HTTP 204 is a success, so there is nothing to retry. The important part is to not read a body. The figure shows the bug and the fix in JavaScript.

1. Check the status before parsing. On HTTP 204, return an empty result instead of calling *response.json()* .
2. Keep the current screen. RFC 9110 says HTTP 204 means the client does not need to change its view (section 15.3.5). Show a short success message.
3. Store new header values, such as a new *ETag* , for the next request.

```
async function request(url, options) {
  const response = await fetch(url, options);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Request failed: ' + response.status);
  if (response.status === 204) return null;
  return response.json();
}
```
